  "title": "Venezuela Wants Foreign Investment. It Still Owes China and Russia Nearly $22 Billion.",
  "deck": "Caracas disclosed $237.5 billion in public debt as it seeks investment to revive its oil industry. A recovery could improve Beijing’s and Moscow’s chances of collecting, but how much they recover depends on the terms Venezuela negotiates.",
  "category": "investigations",
  "categoryLabel": "Investigations",
  "type": "article",
  "author": "EP News",
  "publishedAt": "2026-11-25T22:28:22.476Z",
  "updatedAt": "2026-11-25T22:28:22.476Z",
  "body": [
    "Venezuela is asking foreign investors to help revive its oil industry. Two governments that financed Caracas years ago have a stake in whether that effort succeeds. Venezuela’s new economic framework lists $16.87 billion owed to China and $5.08 billion to Russia, according to Reuters. Together, the two countries account for nearly $22 billion of the $237.5 billion in public debt Caracas disclosed on Tuesday.",
    "Washington has authorized certain oil and gas operations and investments under U.S. sanctions licenses. Venezuela’s debt disclosure shows how that opening could give Beijing and Moscow another route to benefit from a recovery. If the oil industry generates more public revenue, Caracas could have more room to settle old claims, including loans from China and Russia. The terms would depend on separate debt negotiations.",
    "What Caracas owes",
    "The Venezuelan government put public debt at about 242% of GDP at the end of 2025. The total covers a range of obligations, including bonds, arbitration claims and debts between Venezuelan public entities. Those distinctions matter because restructuring talks will have to establish which claims are eligible and how much each group can expect to recover.",
    "The China and Russia figures give a sense of what is at stake for those governments. They leave open when payments might resume, whether either government would accept a reduced amount or longer repayment period, and how those terms would compare with what other creditors receive.",
    "How much can oil pay for?",
    "The government projects crude production will rise from about 1.21 million barrels a day in 2026 to 2.91 million in 2036. Its plan counts on about $160 billion in oil investment over that period, with international partners expected to supply the larger share. Those figures are assumptions in Caracas’s plan. Actual investment and production will determine how much additional money the government can count on.",
    "Any added oil revenue would face competing demands. Venezuela’s recovery plan includes urgent reconstruction and investment in public services. The government also projects that the public sector will continue to spend more than it takes in, before interest payments, through 2030. What is available for creditors will depend on what remains after those demands and the cost of keeping oil production growing.",
    "The next test is the repayment plan",
    "Vice Finance Minister Christiam Hernández said Wednesday that the government expects to publish a debt sustainability analysis in the coming days or weeks. That analysis should give creditors a clearer picture of what Venezuela can afford to repay. The terms offered to China and Russia will show whether they can recover more of what they are owed as Venezuela’s oil industry reopens, and how much room Caracas will have left to finance its own recovery."
  ],
